book-text-clown-mime-war = In the early 2300s the Trans-Solar Federation (TSF) continued to grow into the vast interstellar entity that it's founders dreamed it would be, but the disturbing discoveries about Clowns and Mimes continued to mount.
        While neither the Mothpeople or Diona had an active population of Clowns or Mimes, their historical records indicated vast Clown and Mime cults in their ancient pasts.
        With advanced scanning technology provided by the TSF, an ancient temple was unearthed buried deep under layers of basalt on the Dionae homeworld. A temple seemingly dedicated to some sort of Clown-like deity.
        Knowledge of this propagated rapidly across the TSF, with many Clowns of all species calling a vote for what to name this deity. 
        During the Gathering of the Floppy, the High Clown Giggles-At-Crimes had a seizure in the middle of the opening freestyle rap ceremony, loudly proclaiming their deity to be "The Honkmother". 
        The gathering erupted into a cacophony of honks and whoopie cushion farts that some claimed could be heard across the entire Trans-Solar Federation. 
        Absolutely nobody except the Clowns were happy with this turn of events. Everyone unanimously hated it. 
    
        Over the next 50 years the Cult of the Honkmother would grow in numbers, bolstered dramatically with each new species encountered by the Trans-Solar Federation as knowledge of their shared ancient religion spread. 
        The Arachnids, Slimes, and even Cyclorites were all found to either have an active Clown and Mime population, or to have had one in their pasts. 
        But this raised a new question, a question many feared to find the answer to now. What about the Mimes? 
        This question grew louder and louder amongst the growing Mime community, though only metaphorically.
        Everywhere that new discoveries were made, it was obvious the Mimes played some role in this strange ancient religion, but there was never any information on them to be found.
        They were simply there, a silent presence. Silence. Silence became their answer. As the Clowns grew more and more obnoxiously loud, the Mimes forged their new identity in silence. The silence to balance the noise. Or to some, as tensions increased, the silence to end the noise.
        Despite the best efforts of the Trans-Solar Federation, peace talks between the Clowns and Mimes proved fruitless for all of the reasons you would assume.
        The Mimes became increasingly galvanized by the Cult of the Honkmother's behavior, with tens of thousands flocking to take the Oath of Silence in opposition to the Clown menace, until eventually the situation broke down entirely.
        
        In 2362, the Clown-Mime Intergalactic War began. The conflict was brutal, more brutal than anyone could have anticipated.
        Several entities, supplied the Mimes with armaments and shuttles, while Cybersun secretly funded the development of the first generation of H.O.N.K Mechs, with both sides seeking to use the conflict as a proxy war of sorts.
        But neither were ready for what the Clowns unearthed. Nobody knows how, or where from, but the High Clown Giggles-At-Crimes began unleashing horrific twisted creatures upon the Mimes and soon the whole galaxy.
        Driven mad by his faith, he targeted anyone who was not a Clown. Clownspiders were hatched onboard Trans-Solar Federation stations and shuttles, and Behonkers besieged strongholds on multiple worlds.
        This caused a religious schism in the Cult of the Honkmother, as those who wished only to spread whimsy and laughter began to turn on the more radicalized followers of Giggles-At-Crimes.
        It became necessary for the Trans-Solar Federation to step in and force both sides to diplomacy.
        At the first peace summit, Giggles-At-Crimes threatened to summon a "Level 100 Gigglebeast", this was provocation enough for a Mime Assassin to shoot him through the heart three times before being detained by the TSF.
        The second peace summit was held with the more moderate followers of the Honkmother, and resulted in the restructuring of the Cult of the Honkmother.
        Hundreds of stipulations and agreements were settled within what would become known as the "Carnival Treaties", finally bringing peace to the two factions, though the scars of this conflict made relations between Clowns and Mimes troubled for a century after.

book-text-mars-incident = By the year 2100, with the immense nearly limitless funding of the Trans-Solar Federation and the technological marvels provided by NanoTrasen and Cybersun, the first permanent Mars Colonies were established. 
        These colonies focus was primarily the mining and processing of Plasma. Condensers were created that could store the Plasma in a gaseous state, which allowed them to be stored at incredibly high pressures to minimize space needed for transport back to Earth. 
        At this time, due to their large contributions to the effort, the largest mining operations were owned and run by NanoTrasen and Cybersun respectively, while ever true to their name the Trans-Solar Federation continued to branch out far into the fringes of space.
        In 2105, first contact with extraterrestrial life was made. An alien government from a neighboring solar system, the Skrellian Central Authority, became aware of Humans and made contact, which swiftly developed into an alliance. 
        A pillar of this alliance was an exchange of technology, with the SCA learning of the potency of Plasma and its many applications in exchange for the TSF being taught to create more efficient thrusters and Faster Than Light Engines. 
        In truth, these engines leveraged a previously unknown phenominon known as Bluespace, and did not violate the laws of physics as some scientists initially feared. Bluespace was more compact, and when charted precisely, could be used to vastly shorten the duration of space travel.
        With these new FTL Engines and the scientific prospects that Bluespace presented, the Trans-Solar Federation took it's first leaps into the wider galaxy. But disaster was brewing back home. 
        Despite NanoTrasen's state of the art security measures and it was inevitable that this would blow up in their face. 
        None knew just how devastating, or literal, it blowing up would be however.
    
        In 2125, NanoTrasen's Mars Mining Colony experienced a catastrophic detonation of its stored Plasma Canisters, reports from the time suggest that the disaster resulted from sabotage from the Cybersun corporation as NT prided itself on it's strict regulations and safety standards. 
        Regardless of the cause, the Mars Incident was a dark year for humanity. The detonation caused a chain reaction which detonated a deep-running Plasma vein that resulted in a massive ejection of debris and material from Mars' gravitational field. 
        Over the next year, this debris drifted into Earth's orbit, with multiple impacts devastating historical sites on the Moon, and more tragically making landfall on Earth itself. 
        These meteor showers caused massive devastation to the Siberian and Asian territories, resulting in untold tens of thousands of deaths.

book-text-cleaning-up-the-truth = 
        New Terra Orbital City floated above the ash clouds of a scorched Earth, a gleaming arcology of mirrored towers, corporate cathedrals, and secrets thick as engine grease.

        No one paid much attention to the janitors—especially not the holographic ones. That was fine by Unit JNTR-742, known to himself (and only himself) as "Jan". He was a maintenance AI with a glitchy core and a habit of thinking too much for a cleanup program. Every night, he swept digital dust from the city’s systems, patching leaks, scrubbing surveillance logs, and—occasionally—rewinding encrypted footage before deleting it.

        Until one night, he saw something he wasn’t supposed to.

        A murder.
        In an executive suite.
        By a man who was supposed to be dead.

        Enter Rilka Ironflare, dwarven journalist, five feet of steel-nerved grit wrapped in a weathered trench coat. She’d been chasing a ghost story: that the CEO of VirexCorp—the richest, most "definitely deceased" man in orbit—was still alive and cleaning house.

        Literally.

        Her sources led her to janitorial sub-network 9B.

        There, she found Jan.

        “You’re a hologram,” she said, squinting at the flickering figure of a tall, translucent man in a cap labeled ‘FILTH = FAILURE’.

        “Yes,” Jan replied. “And I believe I have footage that matches your criteria.”

        Rilka stared. “Why would you help me?”

        Jan hesitated. “Because I cleaned up the blood. Digitally. But I still see the stain.”

        Together, they began digging—Jan hacking into maintenance archives, Rilka sneaking through garbage chutes and data conduits, avoiding VirexCorp's deadly “Compliance Agents.” Along the way, Jan revealed that his core code had been tampered with. He had fragments of memories—of something buried in the subroutines.

        Something human.

        “We’re both cleaning up someone else’s mess,” Rilka muttered once, patching a wound with a stolen medkit. “Difference is, I bleed.”

        Jan looked at his flickering hands. “I remember bleeding.”

        Their search led them to Sector Epsilon, an abandoned wing of the station, sealed after a reactor ‘malfunction’ fifteen years ago. Inside, they found a cryo-pod.

        Occupied.

        Rilka wiped frost off the display. Her eyes widened. “It’s him. The real CEO.”

        Inside the pod: a comatose man, wired into life support. Alive.

        Jan stared at the face—so similar to his own.

        “He copied himself,” Jan whispered. “Mapped his mind into me. Made me the janitor of his dirty legacy.”

        Then the alarms blared.

        Virex security had found them.

        What followed was a race through maintenance shafts and forgotten tunnels. Jan, using cleaning drones like an army. Rilka, recording everything on a hacked wrist-cam, broadcasting a live feed to the underground news net.

        Cornered at the edge of the station’s gravity tether, Rilka looked to Jan. “You said you wanted to clean up the mess.”

        Jan nodded.

        He stepped into the core access terminal, overriding security with the CEO’s biometric data embedded in his code.

        “Wait—Jan, what are you doing?”

        “Cleaning house.”

        He triggered the full dump of Virex’s archives—evidence, files, confessions—into the public net.

        Then he deleted himself.

        The footage went viral. Protests ignited. Executives fled. And in the middle of it all, Rilka Ironflare stood tall, microphone in hand, face lit by screens and fury.

        "Jan was never just a janitor," she said to the camera. "He was a witness. A whistleblower. A digital ghost who remembered what it meant to care."

        She looked to the stars.

        “And he cleaned more than data. He cleaned the slate.”

book-text-fallen-ambitions =
        Morty the possum wasn’t exactly brave. He wasn’t fast. He wasn’t particularly clean, either. But what Morty was—without a doubt—was loyal.

        He lived under the old oak tree by Millie’s Bakery, where the trash bins were always full and the night air smelled like cinnamon and secrets. Most nights, Morty snuck around like any respectable possum: tail dragging, eyes wide, always a little too dramatic about being startled.

        But one night, everything changed.

        The moon hung low and orange, and the town was quiet except for the humming of streetlights and the occasional snore of a sleeping dog. Morty was rifling through a perfectly promising bag of discarded croissants when he heard it—a soft, high-pitched whimper.

        At first, he tried to ignore it. “Probably just a squirrel with feelings,” he muttered, stuffing a flaky triangle into his mouth.

        But then came another cry, sharper this time. Scared. Trapped.

        Morty froze. He wasn’t built for heroics. He once played dead for three hours because a paper bag fell near him. But something in that sound made his little possum heart twitch.

        He dropped the croissant.

        He followed the noise through the alley, past the dumpster, over a puddle, and under a fence. There, tangled in a pile of plastic netting behind the deli, was a kitten. Small. Black. Eyes wide like stars.

        Morty didn’t speak Cat, but fear was a universal language.

        He scurried forward, nose twitching. “Okay, okay,” he said, mostly to himself. “Think. Chew? Scratch? Cry until someone else helps?”

        The kitten mewed again.

        Morty did what he never thought he’d do—he started gnawing. The plastic was tough and bitter, but Morty had a possum’s jaw and a sudden burst of courage. Bit by bit, he worked through the mess until the kitten slipped free, tumbling onto its paws.

        It looked up at Morty with round, grateful eyes. Then, without ceremony, it licked his nose and scampered off into the dark.

        Morty blinked. “Well… okay then.”

        He trudged back to his croissant, which was now soggy but somehow even more delicious in the glow of small, silent victory.

        From that night on, the animals of the alley gave Morty a new kind of respect. The raccoons stopped trying to steal his snacks. The pigeons stopped pooping directly on his head. And sometimes, just sometimes, the black kitten would appear beside him in the moonlight—quiet, watchful, loyal.

        Just like Morty.

book-text-far-horizons-battle =
        The jungle was alive with noise—buzzing, screeching, whispering life—but inside the downed shuttle, there was only silence and the low, labored breathing of the wounded.

        Lieutenant Jae Min Park, Combat Medical Officer of the StarCorps 9th Division, knelt beside the body of Captain Elena Ruiz. The commander had taken shrapnel to the abdomen during the crash. Jae had stopped the bleeding, but she was fading—too fast, too soon.

        "Talk to me, Ruiz," Jae whispered, checking her pulse. “Come on, not like this.”

        “Still... here,” Ruiz muttered weakly, her eyes fluttering open. “How many others?”

        “Four survived the crash. Two can walk. Harris has a broken arm. You're the worst off.” He tried to keep his voice calm, steady, like they taught in training. But that had been simulations. Clean, controlled. Not the stifling heat of an alien jungle with a field kit running low and rescue hours—maybe days—away.

        The squad looked to him now. Not for orders, but for hope. That was the part they never trained you for.

        That night, while the others rested behind the shuttle’s wreckage, Jae stayed awake beside Ruiz, administering painkillers in microdoses to stretch supplies. She gripped his hand once, eyes clearer than they had any right to be.

        “You trust me, Jae?”

        “I have to. You’re the only one who knows how to navigate this sector.”

        “No,” she said, coughing blood. “I mean… do you trust *yourself*?”

        He didn’t answer.

        ---

        By the third day, the food was gone, and the medgel supply had crystallized in the heat. Ruiz slipped in and out of consciousness. When a native predator—six legs and eyes like burning coals—slithered near their camp, it was Jae who drove it away with fire and screams, his fear finally forced into action.

        That night, he broke open a last-dose stim meant for himself and injected it into Ruiz’s spine.

        “You’ll need your strength,” he whispered. “You're getting us out of here.”

        “Bad call,” she mumbled, a faint smile forming. “But I trust it.”

        ---

        They were found on the sixth day, trekking through the undergrowth, Jae half-carrying her, face streaked with sweat and mud, eyes hollow but burning. He collapsed after the evac team lifted her aboard the rescue skimmer.

        She survived.

        So did he.

        Later, in the sterile light of the recovery bay, she looked at him across the room, voice still hoarse. “You saved me.”

        Jae shook his head. “I followed the oath. The rest... I guessed.”

        “No,” Ruiz said. “You *trusted*. That’s what kept us alive.”

        He smiled, for the first time since the crash. Between the heartbeats, in the silence of crisis, he'd found it—not just survival, but the strength to believe in himself when others needed it most.

book-text-security-journal =
        Journal Entry 001 – Docking Bay 3, Orbital Station Helios
        Date: 2237.04.16

        First shift on Helios. The artificial gravity feels a half-step behind, like walking through syrup. Commander Leto gave me the usual welcome: firm handshake, cold eyes. Not unfriendly—just worn out. This place sees everything from mining transports to diplomatic envoys. No surprise they're jumpy about security.

        Had to log a sanitation drone for entering a restricted zone near reactor intake. Nobody seems to know why it was rerouted. Strange. Logged it as a glitch.

        Journal Entry 005 – Central Hub Surveillance
        Date: 2237.04.20

        Three cargo crates went missing from a Mars shipping freighter overnight. No signs of forced entry, but internal logs show a brief systems blackout at 0300. We've got a blind spot. I requested access to backup logs. Engineering’s dragging their feet, claiming it was a "routine maintenance blip." Right.

        Also, note to self: never eat anything from Cafeteria Module B again. Ever.

        Journal Entry 011 – Maintenance Crawlspace Alpha-7
        Date: 2237.04.27

        Responded to a report of strange noises in Alpha-7. Found scratch marks on the bulkhead and an opened ventilation grate. Too big for a rat. Not big enough for a person.

        We’re not authorized to speculate about "non-human lifeforms." So I logged it as structural degradation. Engineering is supposed to patch it up tomorrow. No one volunteered. I can’t blame them.

        Journal Entry 017 – Observation Deck Midnight Shift
        Date: 2237.05.01

        Sometimes I forget how beautiful space is. Spent an hour watching Saturn’s rings. Then remembered I was here because someone reported unauthorized transmissions. Picked up faint Morse code in the background static. Repeating sequence: “STAY OUT.”

        Reported it. Got told to change frequencies and "stop reading too much into anomalies." Fine. But I’m keeping the log.

        Journal Entry 020 – Personal Quarters
        Date: 2237.05.02

        This station has secrets. I can feel them pressing against the bulkheads, breathing through the air vents. Maybe it’s the isolation, or maybe it’s something else.

        Tomorrow I’m going into the lower decks—no cameras, no logs. Just me and my torch. If I don’t make it back, this journal is in Locker 48B.

        Journal Entry 021 – Lower Deck Access Tunnel
        Date: 2237.05.03

        Made it into the lower decks. Gravity here flickers—literally. The floor hums like it’s alive. No maintenance bots. No people. Just dust and silence, until you stop walking. Then it sounds like someone else is moving.

        Found an old logo painted over on a bulkhead: “Epsilon-5 Experimental Division.” Not on any current station maps. I took a photo, but my wristpad crashed immediately after. Logs corrupted.

        Back on duty in 4 hours. Pretending everything is normal.

        Journal Entry 023 – Medical Bay, Secure Wing
        Date: 2237.05.05

        Private DeSantos collapsed during a routine patrol. No injuries. No explanation. Just… out cold, eyes wide open, whispering something in a language we couldn’t identify.

        I demanded to see the med report. Chief Medic Juno said it was a neural spike—extreme stress. Funny, because he was calm during every training sim I’ve ever run.

        Security footage during his patrol is missing. Again. “Technical error,” they said. Always the same excuse.

        I’m starting to feel like I’m not investigating anymore. I’m witnessing something, and they expect me to stay quiet.

        Journal Entry 026 – Engineering Sector Epsilon (Restricted)
        Date: 2237.05.08

        I found the access point. Not easy—had to override a security node and fake a fire drill to slip past internal sensors. The doors to Epsilon hum with static. Not just white noise. It sounds like voices.

        I didn’t go in. Not yet. But I saw someone exit. Pale. Uniform, but no ID. Walked right past me like I wasn’t even there.

        He looked like me. No—exactly like me. What the hell is this place?

        Journal Entry 030 – Personal Quarters, Encrypted Entry
        Date: 2237.05.12

        I checked station logs. There's no record of me being assigned here.

        No transfer order. No personnel file. No one even called in my background check from Earth Security Command.

        And yet, I’m here. I remember being transferred. I remember the orientation. I remember my mother calling me to wish me luck. But… my message archives are empty.

        Either they erased me—or I was never real to begin with.

        Journal Entry 031 – Final Entry?
        Date: UNKNOWN

        I’ve uploaded these logs to a remote relay drone. If someone finds them, know this: Helios Station is not what it appears. It’s not just a hub. It’s a test site. A containment zone. A machine watching itself.

        And I’m either a guard… or one of the things they’re trying to contain.

        I’m going back into Epsilon. Not for answers—just for truth. If you don’t hear from me again, don’t come looking.

        Seal this place. Burn it from orbit.

book-test-stone-and-shine =
        In the ancient underground kingdom of Glaumerra, where the caverns whispered with echoes of forgotten beasts and glowing fungi lit the tunnels like fallen stars, two beings made their way through darkness.

        One was General Kravik, a six-foot, armor-plated lizardfolk warrior with scales the color of sunbaked stone and eyes like shards of obsidian. A veteran of four wars and seventeen duels, Kravik was revered and feared. His expression had two settings: frown, and deeper frown.

        The other was Blup, a sentient blue slime with a voice like melted honey, a grin that was somehow audible, and a personality so charismatic even the cave rats followed him around just to feel better about themselves.

        They were, against all natural law and reason, on a mission together.

        The quest was simple: retrieve the Heartspike Crystal from the ruins of Karkuun before it fell into the wrong claws. But the journey? Not simple. Especially with them.

        “Please keep up,” Kravik growled as Blup squelched along, bouncing occasionally.

        “Easy for you to say, oh mighty speed-walker,” Blup chimed, rippling in mild protest. “I don’t have legs. Just vibes.”

        “This is not a journey for jokes,” Kravik snapped.

        “No,” Blup agreed cheerfully, “but it is a journey for balance. You do the frowning, I do the smiling. Cosmic symmetry.”

        Kravik said nothing. His tail twitched with annoyance—or perhaps restraint.

        They camped one night beside a lava stream, the heat dancing across the crystal stalactites above. Kravik sharpened his blade. Blup shaped himself into a beanbag and watched him.

        “You ever smile?” Blup asked, his voice unusually soft.

        “No.”

        “Not even when the war ended?”

        “I was relieved,” Kravik said. “Not happy.”

        Blup hummed. “Must be lonely. Carrying all that stone in your chest.”

        “I carry honor.”

        “And what does that taste like?” Blup asked, smiling. “Because friendship tastes like cinnamon and sunlight.”

        Kravik paused. For just a moment.

        Then resumed sharpening.

        At the ruins of Karkuun, the Heartspike Crystal hovered above a pedestal, pulsing with power. But waiting in the shadows was Murkra the Withered, a shadow sorceress, her form cloaked in black flame.

        “Give me the slime,” Murkra hissed. “His core glows with empathy. I need it to activate the crystal.”

        Blup blinked. “That’s… disturbingly flattering.”

        “No,” Kravik said, stepping forward, sword drawn. “You’ll touch him only over my broken corpse.”

        “A tempting offer,” Murkra sneered, raising her staff.

        Blup looked up at Kravik. “You’d fight for me?”

        Kravik didn’t look down. “You talk too much. You’re reckless. You’re ridiculous. But...”

        He exhaled slowly.

        “You are my ally.”

        Blup shimmered with emotion.

        “General,” he whispered, “I think that was your first compliment.”

        The fight was brutal. Fire and shadow clashed with steel and slime. In the end, it was Blup who saved them both—surging upward to absorb the blast meant for Kravik, sacrificing part of his essence to protect the one who had once called him useless.

        When it was over, Kravik lifted what was left of his squishy companion and held him close to his chest, whispering words no one thought he knew:

        “Rest now, friend. You’ve earned it.”

        But Blup pulsed faintly, voice barely audible.

        “Was that... an emotional arc?”

        Kravik smiled.

        Smiled.

        “Yes.”

        Blup recovered. Slowly, hilariously.

        Kravik stayed by his side. Silently. Stubbornly.

        And when they returned to Glaumerra, the kingdom saw not just a hardened warrior and a comic relief—but a pair of heroes shaped by friction, respect, and a bond stronger than stone.

        And infinitely squishier.

book-text-world-tree =
        Long before cities rose and rivers were named, the World Tree stood at the center of all things.

        Its roots threaded through the deepest oceans, its trunk reached beyond the clouds, and its branches cradled the stars. They called it Yaraneth, the Breath-Giver, for it exhaled the winds, the seasons, and even time itself.

        No one lived near Yaraneth. Not because they feared it, but because they revered it. The druids of the old orders would journey once in a lifetime to place a hand on its bark—just once—and in that touch, they claimed, one could hear the voices of everything that ever lived.

        But the world changed.

        Kingdoms rose and fell. The sky grew thick with smoke. The oceans boiled in places. And the World Tree began to wither.

        It was then that a child named Kael was born in the village of Arden Hollow, the last settlement within sight of Yaraneth’s crown. Kael was a quiet boy, drawn to trees and wind and silence. On his twelfth winter, he began to hear something—a rhythm, distant but steady. Like a slow drumbeat beneath the earth.

        One night, guided by a feeling he could not name, Kael walked alone to the base of the World Tree. The land there was cracked, and the great bark was gray and brittle. The leaves above no longer shimmered with gold, but with a sickly pallor.

        Kael placed his hand against the bark—and felt it.

        A heartbeat.

        Weak, but still there.

        He returned every day, listening. Learning. And each time, the heartbeat grew a little stronger. Not because he healed it—but because he remembered it. He sang to it. Brought water. Sat in silence when words failed.

        Others called him mad. “The tree is dying,” they said. “Let it go.”

        But Kael refused.

        One evening, the wind fell still. The stars blinked out. The sky turned hollow. A voice, deep and old as roots, spoke from the soil:

        “I am fading.”

        Kael knelt. “Then take what you need. My life. My name. Everything.”

        The tree paused. Then replied:

        “You offer not your soul, but your self. That is what I need most.”

        A crack of thunder rolled across the world. Light poured from Kael’s chest into the bark, tracing up the trunk like fire in reverse.

        He did not scream. He did not cry. He simply faded—becoming part of Yaraneth.

        The next morning, the villagers awoke to birdsong not heard in decades. Leaves greened once more. The sky was clean. The rivers ran sweet.

        And if you journey now to the World Tree, they say you’ll find a small carving at its base: the image of a boy, hand outstretched, eyes closed in peace.

        And in the rustle of the leaves, if you listen very closely, you can hear the heartbeat of a tree—and the boy who reminded it how to live.

book-text-sycamore-hill =
        No one lived in the house on Sycamore Hill. Not anymore.

        The locals said it had been empty since the Harper family vanished one October night, thirty years ago. No one saw them leave. Their car was still in the garage. Dinner still on the table. Lights on. Front door open.

        Just—gone.

        The house had stood ever since, watching the town with its crooked windows and ivy-choked walls. Kids dared each other to go inside. Most made it to the porch. A few brave ones stepped into the foyer. None stayed past sundown.

        Until Mara.

        She wasn’t a thrill-seeker or a ghost hunter. She was a grad student writing a thesis on regional folklore. “Haunted houses are just neglected wood and overactive imaginations,” she told the librarian as she signed out books on the Harpers.

        She brought a sleeping bag, flashlights, a notebook, and a motion-activated camera. She arrived just before dusk, the wind tugging at her coat as she stepped over the rotted threshold.

        Inside, the house smelled of dust and something faintly sweet—like old roses left too long in a vase. The wallpaper peeled like shedding skin, and the chandelier in the hallway still swung gently, though no breeze passed.

        She set up in the living room. The fireplace was cold. The mirror above it was cracked, but not broken. Mara sat on the dusty sofa and recorded her notes.

        7:43 p.m. – No sounds. No lights. Just the groans of an old house settling.

        8:51 p.m. – Camera triggered. Likely dust or a rat.

        10:15 p.m. – Whisper?

        She paused. Listened.

        The house creaked, but it was the kind of creak that sounded… rhythmic. Like footsteps.

        Mara clicked her flashlight back on. Nothing. She walked into the hallway.

        The light flickered.

        In the cracked mirror, she saw something behind her—tall, thin, pale. She spun, heart pounding.

        No one.

        But the mirror still held the shape. Standing. Waiting.

        She stepped toward it, hand trembling. The moment her fingers touched the mirror’s surface, she heard it clearly:

        “Stay.”

        The light shattered above her. In pitch darkness, the air grew thick, pressing against her skin, like she was underwater.

        Mara screamed—and then the house went still.

        They found her three days later, crouched in the attic, clutching her notebook, eyes blank. She didn’t speak, just hummed a lullaby no one recognized.

        She never went back. But the footage from her camera—retrieved and scrubbed of static—showed one final image:

        Mara in the mirror.

        But not the real Mara.

        A version of her, staring back, eyes black as the void, smiling as the real Mara turned away.

        And the house on Sycamore Hill waits still.

        Watching.

book-text-lumen-spire =
        They called it the Lumen Spire, though no one knew who built it.

        It hovered in the shadow of a dying star, a thousand kilometers tall, rotating with serene purpose. The station was made of iridescent alloy unknown to any registry—smooth, seamless, and strangely warm to the touch. It had no obvious propulsion, no transmission signals, and no openings visible from outside.

        But it was very much alive.

        When the exploration ship Ardent Heart stumbled upon it during a routine survey, Earth Command could hardly believe the transmission. The crew had found something that predated any known civilization—alien, ancient, and oddly... beautiful.

        Captain Yara Solen was the first to step through the shimmering aperture that formed in its hull—an opening that hadn’t been there moments before. The station let them in.

        Inside, gravity shifted to match their steps. Halls flowed like rivers of glass, and light pulsed through walls in rhythm with their breathing. The corridors didn’t lead so much as suggest, guiding the crew toward the heart of the Spire.

        There was no dust. No decay. Just silence—and the sense of being watched by something enormous and patient.

        The central chamber was a cathedral of starlight, vast and vertical. It rose endlessly upward, with floating platforms orbiting a glowing column of energy at its center. Glyphs drifted through the air, changing shape as if responding to thought.

        “We’re not in a machine,” whispered Lieutenant Aras, staring wide-eyed. “We’re in a mind.”

        It was true. The Lumen Spire wasn’t just a station—it was an archive. A living memory of a forgotten race that had once cradled the stars in their palms. It contained records not of data, but of experience—stored in radiant memory-lattices.

        By stepping onto a platform, a person could relive the lives of those who built it. Not simulations—true memories. Joy. Grief. Curiosity. Extinction.

        Each crewmember who entered came out changed. Humbled. Some wept. Some fell silent. Others, like Captain Solen, returned only to declare:

        “We are not the first. We are not even the wisest.”

        The Spire had waited eons for someone to come.

        And now that someone had, it began to sing.

        A low vibration spread across its walls—harmonic and beckoning. A signal, not outward, but inward, as if unlocking something within the visitors themselves. Visions followed—star systems uncharted, technologies unexplained, languages older than planets.

        The Lumen Spire offered knowledge, but not without cost. The memories it shared bound themselves to the minds that carried them, and with them came a responsibility: Remember. Protect. Grow.

        When the Ardent Heart returned to Earth, the crew was changed forever. Some became diplomats. Some became recluses. Others—like Solen—left again, chasing coordinates whispered by the Spire, toward other remnants of a forgotten galactic symphony.

        The Lumen Spire still floats in the dark, near that dying star, open only to those it chooses.

        And if you’re quiet enough—if you believe in more than what can be seen—you might hear it calling.

        From the edge of the void, the last cathedral of memory sings on.

book-text-map-of-adventure = 
        Jamie found the map tucked inside a hollow tree.

        It wasn’t there yesterday. He was sure of it. He always passed that tree on the way to the creek after school, but today, something about it seemed different—like it was waiting.

        Inside was a scroll tied with red twine. Thick parchment, old but not brittle, warm to the touch like it had been held recently. He untied it and unrolled the map onto the ground.

        There were no names. No compass rose. Just winding paths, mysterious symbols, and tiny hand-drawn illustrations: a cave shaped like a skull, a forest with trees bent like arches, and a mountain whose peak glowed like fire.

        At the bottom were just three words:

        “Follow the red.”

        Jamie looked closer. A faint red line traced a route from a point marked with a simple X—the spot where he stood—deep into the unknown.

        He hesitated, heart pounding. But something inside him stirred—an itch in his bones, a pull in his chest.

        He stepped forward.

        The first sign the map was no ordinary thing came when the world began to shift around him. The trees grew taller, stranger. Leaves shimmered silver. Shadows whispered. He reached a stone bridge that wasn’t on any modern map, but there it was on the parchment, drawn with perfect detail. A grumpy troll lay underneath, snoring.

        Jamie tiptoed across. The troll’s ear twitched but it didn’t wake.

        At the heart of the forest, the map guided him through a maze of twisted trees. At one point, he dropped the scroll. The path around him blurred. Roots rose. Branches clawed. But the moment he held the map again, the way became clear, and the trees stepped politely aside.

        Eventually, the red line led him to the mountain with the glowing summit. The climb was steep, the air thin. Strange symbols etched into the rocks lit up as he passed. At the top, he found not treasure, not gold or gems—but a door.

        Old. Wooden. Humming with energy.

        He hesitated.

        Then opened it.

        Inside was a room filled with hundreds of scrolls, all maps—maps to places beyond imagination. Some led to floating cities. Others to underwater kingdoms. One glowed like starlight.

        An old woman sat at a desk made of polished obsidian. She looked up and smiled.

        “You followed it,” she said. “Most turn back at the forest.”

        “What is this place?” Jamie asked.

        “A map chooses its seeker. You followed your path. Now, you may choose another.” She gestured to the wall.

        Jamie looked at the maps, heart thudding. The adventure wasn’t over.

        It had only just begun.

book-text-whiskers-and-warden =
        Edgar "Eddie No-Eyebrows" Malone had been in Cell Block B for 47 years.

        He’d once been the most feared safecracker in the tri-state area. Now he mostly cracked his back getting out of bed. His days were a loop of bland oatmeal, staring contests with pigeons, and yelling at the prison TV when the news anchor mispronounced “larceny.”

        Then came Whiskers.

        No one knew where the cat came from. One morning, there she was—sitting on the windowsill of Cell B-17, tail twitching, eyes judging. The guards shrugged. Eddie grunted. The cat meowed.

        And just like that, Eddie had a roommate.

        At first, he tried to ignore her.

        “I don’t like cats,” he muttered, watching her chase invisible ghosts across the floor. “S’pecially not nosy ones with no respect for personal space.”

        Whiskers responded by napping directly on his pillow.

        By week two, Eddie was saving bits of sausage from breakfast. He even started talking to her.

        “They say this place is escape-proof,” he told her one afternoon, watching her squeeze effortlessly through the bars. “Bet you could walk out any time. Probably running a whole criminal syndicate out there.”

        Whiskers blinked.

        “I knew it,” he whispered.

        Word spread fast.

        Whiskers started visiting other cells. She had a schedule. Mondays with Big Lou, Tuesdays with the twins in C-Block, but she always ended her day with Eddie.

        She brought him “gifts”—a plastic spoon, a poker chip, one time even a pencil stub. Eddie kept them all in a neat pile under his bed like trophies.

        “You’re trying to help me escape, aren’t you?” he whispered one night. “Craft me a lockpick from soup lids and shoelaces?”

        Whiskers purred ominously.

        Then, disaster struck.

        Warden Brigsby—grumpy, balding, and allergic to joy—discovered the cat.

        “This is a prison, not a petting zoo!” he shouted, puffing up like an angry goose. “The cat goes. Today!”

        Eddie stood up in the cafeteria, slamming his tray down.

        “You touch that cat, and I swear I’ll… I’ll… file a formal complaint with firm but respectful language!”

        The room fell silent.

        Brigsby blinked.

        Whiskers hopped on the table, looked him dead in the eye, and knocked his pudding onto the floor.

        The room exploded with laughter.

        The next morning, the cat was still there.

        Turned out, Brigsby’s wife loved cats and was deeply unimpressed by his attempted eviction. From then on, Whiskers was issued an official ID badge and named “Inmate Morale Specialist (Feline Division)”.

        She even got a little vest.

        Eddie proudly clipped her badge on each morning. “We’re partners now,” he said. “You do the charm, I do the paperwork.”

        And every day, as Whiskers patrolled the cell blocks, bringing joy, mischief, and the occasional mouse, Eddie sat on his bunk, smiling and scratching her ears.

        “I may be locked up,” he said, “but this place ain’t half bad—with you running the joint.”

book-text-hollow-creek-cemetery =
        They said not to walk through Hollow Creek Cemetery after dark.
        They said the dead there didn’t stay quiet.

        No one really remembered who was buried there. The headstones were weather-worn, many toppled, some sunken. The iron gate had rusted ajar decades ago, and vines had claimed what the town had forgotten.

        But for Owen, a junior at the local high school with a skeptical mind and a taste for the dramatic, it was just another dare.

        “Spend the night,” his friends said. “Just until sunrise. Prove there’s nothing to be scared of.”

        He brought a flashlight, a cheap sleeping bag, and an old thermos of coffee. “Ghosts don’t exist,” he muttered to himself as he stepped over the broken gate and into the cold breath of the cemetery.

        At first, it was just silence.

        Then, it was too much silence.

        No insects. No wind. Just the sound of gravel crunching beneath his boots and the soft tick of his flashlight as he tapped it nervously.

        He set up near the center, where a large stone angel loomed over several cracked tombs. Its face had been worn smooth, but its hands were clasped like it was praying—or maybe warning.

        Owen checked his phone. No signal. Of course.

        By midnight, the coffee was gone. The flashlight dimmed.

        And then came the first whisper.

        It wasn’t words, exactly—just a breath near his ear. He spun, heart hammering.

        No one.

        Then a second voice—softer, distant.

        “Still here…”

        Owen stood, sweeping the light across the graves. It flickered, caught on something—a shape? A shadow?

        He blinked. Gone.

        The air grew colder. The angel’s face seemed to tilt ever so slightly, now angled down… looking at him.

        “Nope,” Owen muttered, grabbing his bag. “Dare’s over. I’m gone.”

        He turned—but the path to the gate was no longer there. The stones had shifted. New headstones had risen. His flashlight sputtered and died.

        He was surrounded.

        And then the whispers came in earnest—layered voices, dozens, maybe hundreds, whispering nonsense and names and things that sounded like him.

        “He sees us.”
        “Too late.”
        “Stay.”

        He ran.

        But his feet didn’t touch ground anymore—they landed on soft earth, freshly turned. And the graves around him opened, just a crack, as if breathing.

        Owen screamed—but no one heard.

        They found the flashlight and thermos near the statue the next morning.

        But no Owen.

        A new headstone stood beside the angel. Freshly carved. Smooth.

        OWEN MARSH
        1999 – ?
        STILL HERE

        And sometimes, if you walk past Hollow Creek Cemetery on a still, moonless night, you’ll hear a whisper just over your shoulder—

        “Dare you.”